#!/bin/bash
# Rewrite the login screen when the network changes
# (SPEC.md §spec:console-display).
#
# §req:success-criteria 21: attaching a cable, joining a network, or a new
# address arriving from the router is reflected on the screen without a reboot
# and without anyone logging in. NetworkManager is what knows those things
# happened, and a dispatcher script is how it says so.
#
# THIS SCRIPT DOES NOT GENERATE ANYTHING, and that is the point. Dispatcher
# scripts here run one at a time, and one that runs too long is killed. The
# generator asks NetworkManager for the machine's devices over D-Bus, so running
# it inline would hold every other dispatcher script on the machine behind a
# round trip to the service that just called us - and be killed mid-write if
# that service is busy. NetworkManager's own process does not block on us
# (up, down and dhcp*-change are dispatched asynchronously; only pre-up and
# pre-down make it wait), so the cost is to the queue, not to the network.
# Handing the work to systemd puts it outside that queue entirely.
#
# `restart`, not `start`: systemd satisfies a start request with a job that is
# already queued, so a burst of events - which is what plugging in a cable
# actually produces - could drop the last one. The last one is the one that
# describes where the machine ended up.
#
# WHY dhcp4-change AND dhcp6-change. The platform's own dispatcher handles only
# up and down, and does not need more: its line is an agetty escape that agetty
# re-expands from the kernel every time it draws. Ours holds a literal address,
# so a renewed lease that changes the address reaches the screen only through
# these events.
#
# This lives in /usr/lib rather than /etc for the same reason the firewall zone
# does: it belongs to the image, and an operator can shadow it by name in /etc
# without their change and the image fighting each other.

# NetworkManager passes the interface as $1 and the event as $2. The interface
# is not used: the generator re-reads every device anyway, because a cable going
# down changes which OTHER device the machine should be reached on.
action="${2-}"

case "${action}" in
    up|down|dhcp4-change|dhcp6-change)
        systemctl restart --no-block kantainer-console-network.service
        ;;
esac

# Never fail. A non-zero exit here is logged by NetworkManager against an event
# that has nothing to do with this display, and nothing above this line is worth
# reporting a network failure for.
exit 0
